+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
+//
+// The integrated GBAs' screens and buttons in a browser: http://<console>:6771/ (one GBA, ?p=1-4)
+// and /multi (several at once). The page and server are Porpoise's (its gba_stream), in PS5-ECAM's
+// remote touch screen's look, with its WebSockets (GBAWebSocket.cpp): a WebSocket each for the
+// pictures (/video) and the buttons (/input).
+//
+// The GBAs run in-process (HW::GBA::Core); this only watches them. Each finished frame goes to a
+// one-slot mailbox per GBA (the newest wins) while someone watches; a thread packs it losslessly
+// (the GBA's 15-bit colour: each pixel XOR the one above, then PackBits-style runs, 'GBA2') and
+// each browser gets the newest packed picture, up to eight ahead of its "ack"s, so a slow round
+// trip does not cap the rate: up to 60 a second, or what the page asks for ("fps 30"). The page's
+// buttons come back as a key mask that SI_DeviceGBAEmu ORs into the GBA's keys (released after two
+// seconds of silence), and its Reset / Disconnect buttons as the same requests the controller's
+// GBA buttons make. Nothing here can make the emulation wait: the GBA thread only takes a mutex
+// for a memcpy-sized copy. Plain POSIX sockets (SO_REUSEADDR: the port is free again at once).
+// Messages are Dolphin's (SERIALINTERFACE: errors and warnings always, the rest at Info / Debug).
+//
+// A frontend that maps the GBA's buttons and shows its screen itself (Porpoise) calls the plain C
+// functions at the end, looked up in the core by name.
+
